# Longwave release signers — the keys allowed to sign a release's SHA256SUMS.
#
# ssh-keygen allowed_signers format, so this same file works three ways: as the -f argument to
# `ssh-keygen -Y verify` (scripts/verify-release.sh), as the pin list this app parses
# (release-trust.js), and as something a human can read.
#
# TWO KEYS, ON PURPOSE — this is the answer to losing one.
#
#   ixion@YubiKey5-gpg   the everyday signer. Lives on a YubiKey; needs the card PIN and a
#                        physical touch, so it cannot be used by anything automated, including
#                        CI, and cannot be copied off the card by anything at all.
#   ixion@SecureBackup   an offline ed25519 key, kept out of any machine's normal reach and
#                        used for exactly two things: signing releases if the YubiKey is lost
#                        or destroyed, and rotating this list when that happens.
#
# Both are trusted equally, from the first release that ships this file. That ordering is the
# whole point: a recovery key added AFTER the key it is meant to recover from is worthless,
# because every already-installed copy of the app would only trust the key that is gone, and
# the update that introduced the new key could never be verified by anything already out
# there. So the backup is pinned now, while it is not needed.
#
# These are the same two signers as the ssh-keys-updater manifest in illixion.github.io
# (updater/pinned_signers) — deliberately, so there is one pair of keys to protect and one
# recovery drill to remember rather than a separate set per project.
#
# Rotating a signer: sign the release that changes this file with the OTHER key, and say so in
# the release notes. Anyone still on an older build verifies with what they already pinned;
# anyone who takes that update pins the new list.

